ough the same pipeline\n  - evidence: Image figures/overview_pipeline.png; figure_manifest.json entry overview_pipeline (fallback_reason \"generate-concept-figure exited non-zero (rc=1)\", source_ids []); validation_report.json error \"Missing required figure from strategy: overview_pipeline\".\n  - requested_fix: Remove the overview figure, or replace it with a rendered diagram that actually shows the described pipeline. Do not describe a pipeline diagram that is not present.\n- `si-alternative-ridge-residual` (Supplementary Information, Selection context): The artifacts identify the alternative only by the title \"Resistance-calibrated Matthews predictor for small-graph cover time\". \"With a ridge residual\" appears nowhere in paper_data.json, claims_grounding.json or the source evidence. source_evidence/ contains only the baseline and studied program, with no source for the alternative, so this implementation detail is unsupported.\n  - claim: The selection procedure considered one alternative candidate, a resistance-calibrated Matthews-style predictor with a ridge residual, and it produced no score\n  - evidence: paper_data.json idea_titles (single title); claims_grounding.json claim on selection context lists only counts and scores, with no description of the alternative; source_evidence/downstream/{baseline,program} are identical, with no alternative program.\n  - requested_fix: Reduce the description to the grounded title, \"a resistance-calibrated Matthews-style predictor\", and drop \"with a ridge residual\". Keep the statement that it was unscored.\n- `discussion-noise-slowest-families` (Discussion and Limitations, Evaluation scope and label noise): This states as fact that label noise is non-negligible for specific families. The retained evidence has only a median relative SE of 0.029, with no per-graph or per-family SEs and no maximum, as the Results section itself says. The claim also conflicts with the paper's statement that label noise explains only a minor part of the error.\n  - claim: The labels use about 300 walks per graph, so the noise floor is not negligible for the slowest families\n  - evidence: paper_data.json final_evaluation.metrics.mc_rel_se_median = 0.0287 is the only noise statistic; claims_grounding.json has no per-family or per-graph noise claim; paper.tex Monte Carlo uncertainty section says per-graph values were not retained.\n  - requested_fix: Rephrase as an explicit unmeasured possibility, for example \"per-graph standard errors were not retained, so noise on the slowest-covering graphs cannot be assessed\". Remove the assertion that the noise floor is non-negligible for the slowest families.\n# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. The bibliography has three entries (Feige upper and lower bounds, Kiwi et al. on hyperbolic random graphs). The Feige and Kiwi citations are adequate. Three places in the draft need targeted retrieval. No retrieval was run in this stage.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Aleliunas et al. random-walk cover time bound / universal traversal sequences\n- Lovász survey on random walks on graphs\n- Commute-time identity, effective resistance and Matthews-type cover time bounds\n- Ridge regression (Hoerl and Kennard)\n\n## Rewrite targets\n\n### 1. related-work-worst-case-bounds (high, comparator)\n- **Section:** Related Work (also the Introduction)\n- **Anchor:** \"The $2m(n-1)$ bound of Aleliunas et al. follows by summing commute times ... Lovász's survey on random walks on graphs\"\n- **Reason:** The 2m(n-1) bound is the headline comparator. It is attributed only by author name, with no `\\cite`. The Lovász survey is also named without a citation. A reviewer would expect both to be cited directly.\n- **Search topics:** Aleliunas–Karp–Lipton–Lovász–Rackoff, \"Random walks, universal traversal sequences, and the complexity of maze problems\"; Lovász, \"Random walks on graphs: a survey\".\n\n### 2. discussion-resistance-matthews-features (medium, positioning)\n- **Section:** Discussion and Limitations (the Conclusion repeats the point)\n- **Anchor:** \"resistance-based quantities motivated by the commute-time identity and Matthews-type bounds\"\n- **Reason:** The paper's main proposed next step, richer resistance-based features, rests on the commute-time identity and Matthews bounds. Neither has any support in the draft or the bibliography.\n- **Search topics:** Chandra et al., electrical resistance and commute/cover times; Matthews bounds on covering times via maximum hitting time.\n\n### 3. method-ridge-regression-background (low, background)\n- **Section:** Related Work (\"Statistical prediction with cheap features\")\n- **Anchor:** \"Predicting a graph functional from low-dimensional summaries is a standard use of regression\"\n- **Reason:** The paper claims no novelty for the model class, so a single classic ridge reference suffices.\n- **Search topics:** Hoerl and Kennard, \"Ridge regression: biased estimation for nonorthogonal problems\".\n\n## Deliberately not targeted\n- Quantitative claims, the evaluation protocol and the closed-form cover times are grounded in `paper_data.json` and the repository scripts.\n- Broader literatures were skipped (cover times of random regular or Erdős–Rényi graphs, learned graph-property prediction). Covering them would reshape the related-work story rather than fill a thin spot.\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n# Paper Strategy (paper_standalone, ICLR 2026, double-blind)\n\n**Status:** ready (narrow, measurement-style paper; no improvement claim).\n\n## Title (provisional)\nHow Far Can Five Cheap Graph Statistics Go? Log-Linear Cover-Time Prediction on Small Connected Graphs\n\n## Central question\nFor connected graphs with 6<=n<=30, does a ridge model on cheap statistics (n, m, mean degree, max degree, diameter) predict Monte Carlo start-averaged cover time with lower family-macro log error than the generic 2m(n-1) bound?\n\n## Central thesis\nOn the sealed held-out graphs, the five-statistic log-linear ridge predictor (family-macro log MAE 0.181) is far closer to Monte Carlo cover times than the 2m(n-1) bound (1.754), a constant control (0.870) and a label-shuffled control (1.423); median Monte Carlo relative SE is about 0.029. Scope is 12 synthetic families, 24 test graphs. Which families the predictor fails on is *not* answered by the retained evidence.\n\n## Why this framing\n- The frozen selection is the seed baseline (`study.selection_kind=baseline`, investigation `completed_no_winner`, 1 attempt, 0 scored, 1 unscored, search delta 0.0). The studied method *is* the baseline, so no \"improvement over baseline\" is claimed. The comparison that exists is against the 2m(n-1) bound and controls, all in the same sealed final run.\n- Final outcome is `qualified` / `completed` / correct=true (`final.qualification.*`). Candidate diagnostics trust is `unverified`; disclose. No failed gates exist to discuss.\n- The unscored resistance/Matthews variant has no performance evidence. It appears at most as untested future work.\n- The second half of the task's question (error vs graph type) cannot be answered quantitatively: no per-family metrics in `paper_data.json`. Treat as an explicit unknown/limitation, not a failure finding.\n- Search score (-0.1913) and sealed final (-0.1814) are different scopes; never present them as an improvement.\n\n## Edit mode\nNot edit mode (placeholders unfilled, no previous_paper). `prior_paper_story_status` is nominal (\"preserved\"); nothing staged.\n\n## Method groups\n1. Five-statistic ridge predictor (proposed/studied): standardized log n, log m, log mean degree, log max degree, log diameter; ridge lambda=1, unpenalised intercept; bound fallback with no training data.\n2. 2m(n-1) bound (baseline).\n3. Constant control (baseline).\n4. Label-shuffled control (ablation-style negative control).\n5. Relabeling-invariance check (perm_max_rel_dev 0.0; supporting validity check).\n\n## Results focus\n- One direct comparison of family-macro log MAE (lower better); improvement_vs_bound 1.572 = 1.754-0.181.\n- MC uncertainty only via median relative SE 0.0287 (noise-floor context, small vs 0.181).\n- Episodes 201-203 all gave 0.18137: deterministic candidate on fixed graphs/labels; zero spread is NOT sampling uncertainty over graphs. State that graph-draw uncertainty was not measured.\n- Derived ratios (about 1/5 of constant error, about 1/10 of bound error) allowed if labelled arithmetic.\n- Unknowns: per-family, per-size errors.\n\n## Figures\n| id | type | generator | span | width | rationale |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| overview_pipeline | diagram | generate-concept-figure | full width (single-column template, ~5.5in) | 1.0 | graph -> statistics -> ridge -> compare with MC label and bound; aggregation hierarchy; place on page 1-2 |\n| main_comparison | bar, 1 panel, 4 bars | matplotlib | full width | 0.7 | readable labels without shrinking; export PDF + 300 DPI PNG, reference PDF |\n\nNo trajectory figures. No error bars unless grounded.\n\n## Using the 8-9 page body without padding\nEvidence is thin, so space goes to: precise problem framing and why a worst-case bound is loose on small graphs; reimplementable method spec; metric/aggregation design (clipped log error, family-macro mean); a threats-to-validity section (small test set, family composition, deterministic episodes, MC labels, size range, unverified diagnostics); related work (Aleliunas et al., Feige, Lovász; only cite what is in references.bib). Do not invent experiments. If the evidence cannot honestly fill the budget, prefer substantive discussion; flag risk.\n\n## Supplementary plan\n1. Predictor specification and hyperparameters (frozen program source).\n2. Evaluation protocol and full final-metric table (`final.metric.*`).\n3. Episode records and qualification status (`final.evidence.episodes`, `final.qualification.*`).\n4. Search-stage context, clearly separated (`study.*`).\n5. LLM usage statement (ICLR requirement).\n\n## Forbidden headings / phrases\nHeadings: Search Campaign, Iteration History, Milestones, Progression of Attempts, First Valid Result, Experiment A/B, Run Log, What We Tried.\nPhrases: correctness rate, correct and eligible, eligible, combined_score, rubric, worker_01, \"outperforms the baseline\", \"improves over the baseline\", \"state of the art\", \"generalizes to all graphs\", unsupported \"fails on\"/\"reliable on\" family claims.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
