{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YPMjNmTLkgpeXyhDu1SpGY","type":"tool_result","content":"41:This paper studies a practical, measurable alternative. For connected graphs with $6\\le n\\le 30$, can a handful of cheap statistics (here $n$, $m$, mean degree, maximum degree and diameter), combined by a log-linear model fitted on a few dozen labelled graphs, predict cover time more accurately than the generic bound? A secondary question is how the prediction error depends on graph type. We answer the first question quantitatively within one sealed evaluation and state explicitly that the retained evidence does not answer the second.\n49:\\item A threats-to-validity analysis that separates what the evaluation supports (a large gap on this sample, small relative to Monte Carlo label noise) from what it does not (per-family reliability, uncertainty over graph draws, feature attribution, scaling beyond $n=30$) (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}).\n54:\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/overview_pipeline.png}\n78:Figure~\\ref{fig:gap} plots the resulting log overshoot $\\ln[2m(n-1)/C]$. On the path it is essentially constant, about $0.54$ at $n=30$; on the cycle it is exactly $\\ln 4\\approx1.39$ at every $n$; on the star it grows like $\\ln(n/\\ln n)$ to about $2.0$ at $n=30$; and on the complete graph it grows like $\\ln(n^2/\\ln n)$ to about $5.4$ at $n=30$. % @evidence[[closed_form_gap.json#log_overshoot]]\n83:\\includegraphics[width=0.7\\linewidth]{figures/closed_form_gap.pdf}\n140:\\includegraphics[width=0.7\\linewidth]{figures/main_comparison.pdf}\n172:about $2.9\\%$, or roughly one sixth of the ridge log MAE ($0.029/0.181\\approx0.16$; arithmetic). The label noise is thus small relative to the prediction error and very small relative to the bound's error, and the headline gap cannot be explained by label noise. A simple calibration helps: if the predictor were exact and each label were perturbed by a Gaussian relative error with standard deviation equal to this median, the expected absolute log error would be $\\sqrt{2/\\pi}\\times0.029\\approx0.023$, about $13\\%$ of the observed $0.181$. % @evidence[[closed_form_gap.json#noise_floor_mae_gaussian]]\n173:This is an order-of-magnitude statement under a Gaussian approximation, not a measured noise floor, but it indicates that label noise explains only a minor part of the ridge error. Two cautions apply. First, this is a median: the retained record does not include the maximum standard error or the per-graph values, so the noisiest graphs (likely the slowest-covering families) may carry larger relative uncertainty. Second, the standard error treats the walks as independent samples of cover time and characterises only simulation noise; it does not include the variability of which graphs were drawn.\n184:The second research question asks how error depends on graph type. The retained evaluation record reports only family-macro aggregates; per-family and per-size errors were not retained, and we do not attempt to reconstruct them. We can therefore not say that the predictor is reliable on, or fails on, any named family. The aggregation itself offers a bounded statement: since the score is an unweighted mean over $12$ families, a macro value of $0.181$ means the average family-level mean log error is $0.181$, and this is compatible with a mix of families with small errors and a few with larger ones. Which of these holds remains unknown. Arithmetic does give a loose bound: since errors are non-negative, no family mean can exceed $12\\times0.181\\approx2.18$ (a multiplicative factor of about $8.8$), and, with two test graphs per family, no single graph's log error can exceed $24\\times0.181\\approx4.35$ (a factor of about $78$). % @evidence[[closed_form_gap.json#worst_case_family_mean_log_error]]\n189:\\paragraph{What the result supports.} Within one sealed evaluation of $24$ held-out graphs from $12$ synthetic families with $8\\le n\\le 26$, a log-linear model on five cheap statistics tracks Monte Carlo cover times with family-macro log error of $0.181$, far below the $2m(n-1)$ bound ($1.754$), the constant predictor ($0.870$) and the shuffled-target control ($1.423$). The gap to the bound is large relative to Monte Carlo label noise.\n193:\\paragraph{No uncertainty over graph draws.} The only uncertainty reported is Monte Carlo label noise. We did not bootstrap over test graphs, resample training sets, or generate repeated graph draws, so we cannot give a confidence interval for the macro log MAE. The apparent determinism across episodes is not a substitute.\n201:\\paragraph{Evaluation scope and label noise.} The labels use about $300$ walks per graph, so the noise floor is not negligible for the slowest families, and the clipped log error saturates at $10$, which would hide very large failures. Per-graph errors were not retained, so whether any test graph approaches that cap cannot be checked here.\n216:Gap is not an artefact of label noise & Supported (median relative SE $0.029$) \\\\\n308:The configuration studied here is the reference configuration retained by model selection. The selection procedure considered one alternative candidate, a resistance-calibrated Matthews-style predictor with a ridge residual, and it produced no score: zero candidates were scored and one was unscored. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\n309:Unscored candidates are missing evidence rather than measured losses, and we draw no conclusion about that alternative. The selection score of the retained configuration was $-0.1913$ (a negated macro log MAE on the selection test graphs), identical to the reference by construction, so the score difference is $0.0$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.baseline_score]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.search_score_delta]]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afcdc791-b4aa-4206-89ff-ecce3395fcc8","uuid":"3b5b78